   ],
   "rule": {
    "title": "The articular infinitive",
    "body": "With a preposition and the neuter article, the infinitive becomes a clause: ἐν τῷ + infinitive 'while …', διὰ τό 'because …', εἰς τό / πρὸς τό 'in order to', μετὰ τό 'after …', τοῦ + infinitive 'in order to'. Indirect speech can use the infinitive with its subject in the accusative.",
    "examples": [
     {
      "gr": "ἐν τῷ σπείρειν αὐτόν",
      "en": "while he was sowing"
     },
     {
      "gr": "εἰς τὸ σωθῆναι",
      "en": "in order to be saved"
     },
     {
      "gr": "μετὰ τὸ ἐγερθῆναί με",
      "en": "after I am raised"
     }
    ]
   },
